WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:01.968
Special counsel's investigation

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.703
of former President Donald Trump.

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:05.238
Our senior legal affairs

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:06.239
correspondent Paula Reid

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.374
is joining us right now.

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:08.675
Paula, tell us about

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.410
this New York Times report

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.646
and why potentially it is so significant,

00:00:13.947 --> 00:00:15.682
an incredibly significant development

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.417
in the special counsel investigation.

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.019
The New York Times reporting

00:00:19.019 --> 00:00:19.652
that Trump's

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:21.187
former White House chief of staff,

00:00:21.187 --> 00:00:23.089
Mark Meadows, has testified

00:00:23.089 --> 00:00:24.224
before the grand jury

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:25.091
that special counsel

00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:26.459
Jack Smith has been using

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:28.228
in both of his investigations.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:29.662
Well into January six

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.564
and also into the possible mishandling

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.066
of classified documents

00:00:33.066 --> 00:00:34.467
down at Mar a Lago.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:35.435
Now, according to The Times

00:00:35.435 --> 00:00:37.003
reporting, it's unclear

00:00:37.003 --> 00:00:38.004
if he has testified

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.706
in just one of those investigations

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:40.807
or invoke.

00:00:40.807 --> 00:00:42.442
But he is arguably the most

00:00:42.442 --> 00:00:43.643
significant witness

00:00:43.643 --> 00:00:45.812
that the special counsel could speak to,

00:00:45.812 --> 00:00:46.846
certainly in the January

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.048
six investigation.

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:49.082
But it could potentially

00:00:49.082 --> 00:00:50.950
also have valuable information

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.820
in the classified document probe as well.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:55.555
Now, in recent months,

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:56.856
folks around former President

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:58.158
Trump have expressed

00:00:58.158 --> 00:00:59.559
some concerns and puzzlement

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.360
about exactly

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:02.562
what was going on with Mark Meadows,

00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:03.897
because they told us

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:05.331
that they didn't have any communication

00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:06.399
with his legal team.

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:07.901
They were unclear

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.369
what exactly was going on with him.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:10.770
They've even said on our air

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:11.971
that there was no communication

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:13.273
between Trump's legal team

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:14.340
and Meadows legal team,

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:15.842
which raised a lot of questions

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:18.011
about whether he was indeed cooperating

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:20.013
with investigators

00:01:20.013 --> 00:01:20.547
Now, in terms

00:01:20.547 --> 00:01:22.282
of what he can offer on January six,

00:01:22.282 --> 00:01:23.716
you know, the House investigation

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.752
into January six basically concluded

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:27.487
that all roads

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.322
lead to the former White House

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:30.090
chief of staff.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:30.990
All the communication

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:31.391
to the former

00:01:31.391 --> 00:01:32.225
president that day,

00:01:32.225 --> 00:01:33.726
including from his own family,

00:01:33.726 --> 00:01:35.195
went through Mark Meadows.

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:36.062
He would have an incredible

00:01:36.062 --> 00:01:37.530
number of questions

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:39.766
to answer before investigate matters.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:41.835
Now, also, though, on the Mar

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:42.535
a Lago probe,

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:43.770
we learned from our exclusive

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.171
reporting in recent weeks

00:01:45.171 --> 00:01:46.573
that investigators

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:48.041
have an audio recording,

00:01:48.041 --> 00:01:48.641
really key

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:49.576
piece of evidence

00:01:49.576 --> 00:01:50.610
where Trump refers

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.444
to being in possession

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:52.946
of a classified document.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:55.815
And that recording was made by Meadows

00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:56.950
Auto biographers

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.952
who were recording in the room.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.120
Now, in a statement,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:01.054
Meadows lawyers

00:02:01.054 --> 00:02:02.555
provided to the Times,

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.491
they said, quote, Without commenting

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:05.391
on whether or not Mr.

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:06.493
Meadows has testified

00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:07.527
before the grand jury

00:02:07.527 --> 00:02:09.229
or in any proceeding, Mr.

00:02:09.229 --> 00:02:10.964
Meadows has maintained a commitment

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.031
to tell the truth

00:02:12.031 --> 00:02:14.267
or his illegal obligation to do so Wolf.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.503
And I want to just point out that one

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.138
well-placed source has now confirmed

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:19.305
to CNN

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:20.006
this report

00:02:20.006 --> 00:02:21.341
about Mark Meadows testifying

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:23.243
before this grand jury.

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:24.711
What does this tell us, Paula,

00:02:24.711 --> 00:02:26.479
about where the investigation

00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:27.480
is right now?

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:28.314
Well, as we've been reporting

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:29.382
over the past several weeks,

00:02:29.382 --> 00:02:31.084
it appears that this investigation is in

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:32.452
its final phase

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:34.053
based on the types of witnesses,

00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:35.088
the requests that they have,

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:35.388
the fact

00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:36.689
that they're bringing people back.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:37.624
But the thing that we've said

00:02:37.624 --> 00:02:38.491
repeatedly on air

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:39.359
including on your show,

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:39.692
Wolf, is

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.528
we just don't know about Mark Meadows.

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:41.928
Right.

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.396
That is such a significant witness.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:44.364
Once they talk to him,

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:45.431
then we can say for certain

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:46.299
that this is definitely

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.400
in the final phase

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:48.601
and likely wrapping up.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:49.135
But, of course,

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.271
we know that the investigation continues.

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:53.072
Investigators are expected

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:54.741
to speak to at least one more witness

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:56.476
in Florida later this week.

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:58.244
But the fact that they have talked in

00:02:58.244 --> 00:02:59.913
that this has finally been confirmed.

00:02:59.913 --> 00:03:00.547
This is something

00:03:00.547 --> 00:03:01.514
we've been waiting for

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.082
to really understand

00:03:03.082 --> 00:03:04.817
whether they are indeed wrapping up

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:05.885
this investigation.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:06.519
It appears,

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:06.819
again,

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:07.287
this is one of the

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:08.555
most significant witnesses

00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:09.522
they would speak to.

00:03:09.522 --> 00:03:10.890
This is someone that it appears

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:11.791
that they have likely spoken

00:03:11.791 --> 00:03:13.526
to in this final stage

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:14.460
that we're entering now.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:15.528
And they contemplate

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:16.629
whether they will indeed

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:17.597
bring any charges.

00:03:17.597 --> 00:03:18.431
Yeah, let's not forget,

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:19.666
this is a criminal

00:03:19.666 --> 00:03:21.234
investigation of Donald Trump

00:03:21.234 --> 00:03:22.769
that we're watching right now.

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:23.703
Paula, stay with us.

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:25.371
I want to dig deeper with our legal

00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:26.372
and political experts

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.340
who are standing by.

00:03:27.340 --> 00:03:28.808
Eliot, Williams, let me start with you.

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:29.709
How critical

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:31.811
is this Mark Meadows testimony

00:03:31.811 --> 00:03:33.213
before this grand jury?

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:34.514
It's very critical, Wolf,

00:03:34.514 --> 00:03:35.715
and for a big reason.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:36.783
Let's look at the role

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:38.251
that Mark Meadows would have played

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:39.419
in the Trump White House

00:03:39.419 --> 00:03:40.353
as chief of staff.

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:42.755
He would have had access or insight

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:43.890
into political questions

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:44.457
in the White House,

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.192
but also substantive ones.

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:46.926
And so look at the

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:47.860
two different inquiries

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:48.895
that are happening here

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:50.463
to the extent that he could provide

00:03:50.463 --> 00:03:51.764
insight as to January six.

00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:52.165
He could

00:03:52.165 --> 00:03:53.132
he could talk about

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.267
what the president said,

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:55.101
what the president knew.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:56.502
Did the president ever convey

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:58.504
that he knew he lost the election?

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:00.073
What was in the president's mind?

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:01.574
What was his state of mind

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:02.442
around the statements

00:04:02.442 --> 00:04:04.210
he made on January 6th and other,

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.011
you know, activity?

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.045
So there's plenty

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.347
to be gleaned from him there.

00:04:07.347 --> 00:04:09.249
With respect to documents, it's

00:04:09.249 --> 00:04:11.484
an entirely separate investigation.

00:04:11.484 --> 00:04:13.353
But he could also provide insight into

00:04:13.353 --> 00:04:14.254
what did the president

00:04:14.254 --> 00:04:16.256
know was being packed up in boxes

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:16.923
on the final days

00:04:16.923 --> 00:04:18.691
of administration, of the administration

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:20.593
and being sent down to Mar a Lago.

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:21.961
What did the president say

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:24.330
about his right to declassify documents

00:04:24.464 --> 00:04:25.999
or that he knew was in his possession?

00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:26.599
There's plenty

00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:28.568
that can be asked of Mark Meadows, again,

00:04:28.568 --> 00:04:30.603
as both a substantive and political aide.

00:04:30.603 --> 00:04:33.039
He was clearly a critical witness.

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:33.873
He certainly was.

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:36.609
And Carrie Cordero, he was key.

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.612
He was well known in the White House,

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:40.947
obviously the White House chief of staff.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:42.015
But he was key

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:43.249
to various aspects

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:45.018
of both of these investigations.

00:04:45.018 --> 00:04:46.653
Presumably that's why they want to

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:47.553
question him,

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:49.489
the January six insurrection,

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:51.157
as well as the classified documents

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:53.526
that were lying around Mar a Lago.

00:04:53.526 --> 00:04:54.160
That's right.

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.296
And as Elliot and Paul explained,

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.665
because he was had so much access,

00:04:58.665 --> 00:05:00.433
because he was in the position

00:05:00.433 --> 00:05:00.867
that he was,

00:05:00.867 --> 00:05:03.036
he just had firsthand knowledge.

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:05.371
I think the fact that he has testified

00:05:05.371 --> 00:05:06.439
now before the grand jury

00:05:06.439 --> 00:05:07.307
and one of the big questions

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:08.308
I have, Wolf, is

00:05:08.308 --> 00:05:10.510
did he actually testify

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:11.978
from a substantive perspective?

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:14.213
In other words, answer questions

00:05:14.213 --> 00:05:16.282
substantively meaningfully

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.151
for what amount of time,

00:05:18.151 --> 00:05:19.952
how many hours was he in there

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:22.155
or was there a circumstance

00:05:22.155 --> 00:05:23.956
where he would have been in a position

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:25.992
to invoke his own Fifth Amendment right?

00:05:26.025 --> 00:05:26.192
I mean,

00:05:26.192 --> 00:05:27.794
I think one of the big questions

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:28.928
is whether or not

00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:30.463
Mark Meadows faces

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:31.964
any criminal liability

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:34.100
in these broader investigations himself.

00:05:34.334 --> 00:05:36.469
I don't know the answer to that question,

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:38.638
but it would certainly affect

00:05:38.905 --> 00:05:40.173
the length of time

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:42.175
that has gone before he testified

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:43.076
whether or not

00:05:43.076 --> 00:05:45.178
he entered into any kind of

00:05:45.178 --> 00:05:46.879
deal with the government,

00:05:46.879 --> 00:05:48.948
whether or not he had a motivation

00:05:49.148 --> 00:05:51.217
to testify extensively

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:53.052
in his own interests,

00:05:53.052 --> 00:05:54.120
and therefore,

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:55.021
how truthful

00:05:55.021 --> 00:05:56.889
and how forthcoming

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:57.523
he would be

00:05:57.523 --> 00:05:59.158
in his answers

00:05:59.158 --> 00:06:00.393
with respect to whether or not

00:06:00.393 --> 00:06:01.194
they could implicate

00:06:01.194 --> 00:06:02.528
him in anything as well.

00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:04.497
So there's his own personal interests

00:06:04.497 --> 00:06:06.265
and then there's also the relevance

00:06:06.265 --> 00:06:07.800
to the broader investigations.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.001
Let me go back to Paula.

00:06:09.001 --> 00:06:10.603
That is was also linked,

00:06:10.603 --> 00:06:11.304
as we noted,

00:06:11.304 --> 00:06:13.306
that important piece of evidence that you

00:06:13.573 --> 00:06:15.508
and the CNN team reported on.

00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:17.577
Specifically the

00:06:17.577 --> 00:06:19.178
audio recording of Trump

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:21.247
discussing those classified documents

00:06:21.247 --> 00:06:22.382
at Mar a Lago.

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:23.883
Such a critical piece of evidence

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:24.917
for investigators.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:26.185
We learned that on this recording.

00:06:26.185 --> 00:06:28.388
Not only does the former president

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:29.889
suggest that he is in possession

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.457
of a classified document,

00:06:31.457 --> 00:06:33.626
but he also appears to acknowledge

00:06:33.626 --> 00:06:34.761
the limits on his ability

00:06:34.761 --> 00:06:36.262
to declassify documents

00:06:36.262 --> 00:06:37.797
once he left the White House.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:39.031
Now, those comments are significant

00:06:39.031 --> 00:06:40.299
because they really undermine

00:06:40.299 --> 00:06:41.834
a lot of the public defenses

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:43.236
that Trump has put forward,

00:06:43.236 --> 00:06:44.470
put in reporting that story.

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.472
One of our big questions was, okay,

00:06:46.472 --> 00:06:48.808
who was a recording in this room and why?

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:50.076
And through our reporting,

00:06:50.076 --> 00:06:51.811
we learned that during that time

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:53.713
the former president was in the habit

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:55.515
of having journalists, writers

00:06:55.515 --> 00:06:57.583
and anyone working on books a record.

00:06:57.583 --> 00:06:58.718
What he said in addition

00:06:58.718 --> 00:07:00.086
to his aides as well.

00:07:00.086 --> 00:07:01.521
We learned that in the room

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:01.988
during this

00:07:01.988 --> 00:07:03.222
critical conversation

00:07:03.222 --> 00:07:03.990
were two people

00:07:03.990 --> 00:07:06.559
who were working on an odd autobiography

00:07:06.726 --> 00:07:08.161
for Mark Meadows.

00:07:08.161 --> 00:07:08.828
So, again,

00:07:08.828 --> 00:07:11.531
he is tied into this specific case

00:07:11.531 --> 00:07:12.632
now very clearly.

00:07:12.632 --> 00:07:13.633
Other questions

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:15.802
about what other types of recordings

00:07:15.802 --> 00:07:17.103
they could have related to Mark

00:07:17.103 --> 00:07:17.870
Meadows book.

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:19.172
Also, oddly,

00:07:19.172 --> 00:07:20.807
in Mark Meadows autobiography,

00:07:20.807 --> 00:07:22.442
he suggests that he was in this meeting,

00:07:22.442 --> 00:07:23.776
though our reporting suggests,

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:25.077
in fact, that he was not.

00:07:25.077 --> 00:07:26.179
So I think investigators

00:07:26.179 --> 00:07:27.313
would have quite a bit

00:07:27.313 --> 00:07:29.415
to ask Mark Meadows about in the Mar

00:07:29.415 --> 00:07:31.451
a Lago documents investigation.

00:07:31.451 --> 00:07:32.385
And David Chalian,

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:33.953
how big of a blow potentially

00:07:33.953 --> 00:07:36.055
is this to former President Trump?

00:07:36.055 --> 00:07:36.589
Well,

00:07:36.589 --> 00:07:37.423
that all depends on

00:07:37.423 --> 00:07:40.460
what Mark Meadows told the grand jury.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:42.295
But it could,

00:07:42.295 --> 00:07:43.496
I think, back to

00:07:43.496 --> 00:07:45.665
sort of Mark Meadows trajectory

00:07:45.665 --> 00:07:47.767
in this whole notion of investigations

00:07:47.767 --> 00:07:48.601
into Donald Trump.

00:07:48.601 --> 00:07:50.002
Remember when Mark Meadows

00:07:50.002 --> 00:07:52.238
was initially very cooperative

00:07:52.238 --> 00:07:53.573
with the House Select

00:07:53.573 --> 00:07:55.808
Committee looking into January 6th,

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:57.944
and then he totally pulled back from

00:07:57.944 --> 00:07:58.544
that after

00:07:58.544 --> 00:08:00.713
he had shared a bunch

